(I think _wishing for artifacts_ is cheap, so I don't do that, and don't
comment on that.) I'll focus on the DSM argument; though most had already
been said. There's no question that DSM increases early survival rate.
There's in my experience also no question that having light (throughout
the game) increases survival rate. (David assumes that he always has
enough light available; so we disagree here.) Using a random magic lamp
for a chance of a wish means; either you get it and you survive easier,
or you don't get it and you have _to continue as if you had no wish_; but
in the latter case I reduce my overall survival rate. In case one wants to
win every game he should IMO take not only local advantages into account
but weigh them with the options the game presents at many places.

> 
> Amulet of lifesaving obviously doubles your life expectancy, [...]

Huh? - AoLS gives you a second chance, but nothing else. It seems to have
been agreement here that it may help if you play inattentive, or secure
you from insta-deaths (like poison if you don't have the resistanec), and
might help if you are trying to fight some known severe monster (while
being underprepared). Otherwise you will typically be just killed shortly
after that first amulet, because you're not yet perpared to handle the
situations at that stage.

> 
> Find an early wand of wishing and "save up wishes?"

No. With an early WoW I suggested to wish for DSM.

> "Check Sokobon before wishing?" FOOLISHNESS. [...]

Nonsense! With an (additional) 9-12 AC from the dragon mail, you can get
easily to Sokoban; get all the wands and rings, get the price. And if the
price is not what you needed wish for it. Only inexperienecd players would
waste a wish if they know there's a 50% chance to get it just "around the
corner" for free. I'd use the available wish for the next necessary item!

> 
> NEVER wait to use wishes in the early game.

Typically you need more than a single item. Some items have a large general
impact (like huge AC and MR), other a smaller one. Wish for the ones that
have a large impact, and if you have to choose which of the other necessary
items you need take into consideration whether you may get them easily, so
that with minimum risk you can get all, say 5, items with only, say 3, wishes.

Only in case of inexperienced players who don't know where to find what in
the dungeons, and who have not yet obtained a feeling for the actual danger
of existing levels and situations the immediate use all wishes seems to make
sense.

> I once had a magic lamp at the bottom of Sokobon.

(I don't think this is possible as random object; was it s death drop?)

